### Page 7 of 18

```text
I. INTRODUCTION
The City of Cheyenne (City) is seeking proposals from qualified attorneys or firms to
provide counsel in the area of municipal financing options in connection with services related to
the recent passage of a 6th Penny sales tax ballot proposition intended to fUnd the construction of
a new Municipal Court.
The amount authorized by the ballot proposition for this project is
$9,000,000.00.
A copy of the Official Election Ballot is attached to this RFP.
The Municipal
Court construction was authorized in Proposition 1.
II. GENERAL INFORMATION AND INSTRUCTIONS
A.
Who may respond. Attorneys who are currently licensed to practice law in the
State of Wyoming, or law firms including such attorneys, who have significant experience in
public finance and are listed in the Bond Buyer’s directory of municipal bond attorneys (the
“Red Book”) may respond to this RFP.
B.
Public notice. Notice of the RFP will be published on October 3,2017 and
October 10, 2017.
B.
Closing submission date. Proposals must be received by the City no later than
October 20, 2017 at 3:00 p.m. MOT. Proposals must be delivered or mailed; fax or email
submissions will not be accepted. Late submissions shall not be considered.
C.
Proposal submission.
1.
All proposals shall include an original clearly marked as such and four
copies.
The proposal shall be submitted in a sealed envelope clearly marked in the lower left-
hand corner with the following information:
Request for Proposal Number
Date and Time
Sealed Proposal for Municipal Finance Counsel Services
2.
All costs incurred in the preparation of any proposal shall be the
responsibility of the proposer and shall not be reimbursed by the City.
3.
Proposals shall be addressed as follows:
Purchasing Office, Mm 309
Atth: Sylvia Lee Hackl, City Attorney
City of Cheyenne
2101 O’Neil Avenue
Cheyenne, Wyoming 82001
4.
All proposals submitted in response to this request become property of the
City and thus are considered to be public records, and so may be subject to public review. The
laws of Wyoming require that the contents of all proposals shall be placed in the public domain
and be open to inspection by interested parties. Trade secrets or proprietary information that are
recognized as such and are protected by law may be withheld if clearly identified as such in the
proposal. Please mark in bold red letters the term “CONFIDENTIAL” on that part ofthe response
which is confidential. The proposer may be required to submit in writing specific detailed reasons,
2
